First, you will need to create a Discord bot application and add the necessary permissions to it. You can follow the official Discord.js guide for creating a bot and getting the necessary credentials.

Once you have the bot credentials, you can create a Discord.js v14 project and install the necessary packages:

```bash
npm init -y
npm install discord.js dotenv
touch .env
```

Create a `.env` file and add your bot token:

```dotenv
BOT_TOKEN=<your-bot-token>
```

Create a `index.js` file and add the following code:

```javascript
const { Client, GatewayIntentBits, SlashCommandBuilder } = require('discord.js');
require('dotenv').config();

const client = new Client({
  intents: [
    GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
    GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
    GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent,
  ],
});

client.on('ready', () => {
  console.log('Bot is ready!');
});

const banCommand = new SlashCommandBuilder()
  .setName('ban')
  .setDescription('Bans a user from the server.')
  .addUserOption((option) =>
    option
      .setName('user')
      .setDescription('The user to ban.')
      .setRequired(true),
  )
  .addStringOption((option) =>
    option
      .setName('reason')
      .setDescription('The reason for the ban.')
      .setRequired(false),
  );

client.on('interactionCreate', async (interaction) => {
  if (!interaction.isCommand()) return;

  const { commandName, options } = interaction;

  if (commandName === 'ban') {
    const user = options.getUser('user');
    const reason = options.getString('reason') || 'No reason provided';

    await interaction.reply(`Banning ${user.tag} for ${reason}`);

    await interaction.guild.members.ban(user, { reason });
  }
});

client.login(process.env.BOT_TOKEN);
```

Make sure to replace `<your-bot-token>` with your actual Discord bot token.

Run the bot with `node index.js`. Once the bot is ready, you can test the `/ban` command in a Discord channel with the bot's permissions to ban users.

Here's an example implementation of a C++ function that converts Markdown to HTML:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <regex>
#include "Markdown.h"

std::string convertMarkdownToHtml(std::string& markdown) {
    std::stringstream ss;
    Markdown md;

    // Convert Markdown to HTML
    md.parse(markdown);
    md.render(ss);

    return ss.str();
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "Hello **world**! This is a *test*.";

    std::string html = convertMarkdownToHtml(markdown);

    std::cout << html << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

To use this function, you'll need to include the `Markdown.h` header file, which is a simple Markdown parsing library. You can find various implementations of Markdown parsers online, such as the one used in this example (https://github.com/tomstuart/cpp-markdown).

This implementation converts the Markdown string using the `Markdown` class, which is a wrapper around the Markdown parser. The `parse()` function reads the Markdown string and stores it in the parser's internal representation. The `render()` function then converts the Markdown to HTML and writes it to a `stringstream`.

Note that this implementation assumes that the Markdown string is well-formed and does not contain any invalid syntax. You may want to add error handling and validation to your own implementation.

