<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Modal Chatbot</title>
    <style>
      /* Style the modal */
      .modal {
        display: none; /* Hidden by default */
        position: fixed; /* Stay in place */
        z-index: 1; /* Sit on top */
        left: 0;
        top: 0;
        width: 100%; /* Full width */
        height: 100%; /* Full height */
        overflow: auto; /* Enable scroll if needed */
        background-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); /* Fallback color */
        background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9); /* Black w/ opacity */
      }

      /* Style the content inside the modal */
      .modal-content {
        margin: auto;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        max-width: 600px;
        padding: 20px;
        background-color: #f2f2f2;
        color: #333;
      }

      /* Style the close button (X) */
      .close {
        position: absolute;
        top: 15px;
        right: 25px;
        font-size: 40px;
        font-weight: bold;
        color: #f1f1f1;
        cursor: pointer;
      }

      /* Style the messages */
      .message {
        border-radius: 10px;
        padding: 10px;
        margin-bottom: 10px;
      }

      /* Style the bot messages */
      .bot-message {
        background-color: #e6e6e6;
      }

      /* Style the human messages */
      .human-message {
        background-color: #f2f2f2;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <button id="open-modal">Open Chat History</button>

    <!-- The modal -->
    <div id="myModal" class="modal">
      <!-- Modal content -->
      <div class="modal-content">
        <span class="close">&times;</span>
        <div class="message bot-message">Bot: Hello, how can I assist you today?</div>
        <div class="message human-message">Human: I need help finding a good restaurant nearby.</div>
        <div class="message bot-message">Bot: Sure, I can help you with that. What type of cuisine are you interested in?</div>
        <div class="message human-message">Human: I'm looking for Italian food.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <script>
      // Get the modal
      var modal = document.getElementById("myModal");

      // Get the <span> element that closes the modal
      var span = document.getElementsByClassName("close")[0];

      // When the user clicks on <span> (x), close the modal
      span.onclick = function() {
        modal.style.display = "none";
      }

      // When the user clicks anywhere outside of the modal, close it
      window.onclick = function(event) {
        if (event.target == modal) {
          modal.style.display = "none";
        }
      }

      // Open the modal when the button is clicked
      document.getElementById("open-modal").onclick = function() {
        modal.style.display = "block";
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

This code creates a modal with a fake chat history. When the user clicks the "Open Chat History" button, the modal appears with four messages: two from the bot and two from the human. The bot messages have a gray background, while the human messages have a light gray background. The close button (X) can be clicked to close the modal.

In TypeScript, you can use a technique called "type narrowing" to tell the type system that a value may have multiple types, but in practice, it will only have one specific type.

Type narrowing is achieved through type guards, which are expressions that help TypeScript determine the type of a value at runtime. When a value is narrowed down to a specific type, TypeScript will use that type for the rest of the code block.

Here's an example:

```typescript
let value: string | number = "hello";

if (typeof value === "string") {
  // TypeScript knows that `value` is a string here
  console.log(value.length);
} else {
  // TypeScript knows that `value` is a number here
  console.log(Math.floor(value));
}
```

In this example, the `value` variable is initialized with a `string` value, but it could also be a `number`. The `typeof` operator is used as a type guard to narrow down the type of `value` to `string` inside the first `if` block.

TypeScript now knows that `value` is a `string`, so it allows you to access string-specific properties like `.length`. Similarly, TypeScript knows that `value` is a `number` inside the second `else` block, so it allows you to call `.floor()` on it.

By using type guards, you can tell TypeScript that a value may have multiple types, but in practice, it will only have one specific type, allowing you to take advantage of type safety while still being flexible with your code.

