In Java, the `==` operator compares the references of two objects, while the `.equals()` method compares the values of two objects. Therefore, when comparing strings in Java, it is recommended to use the `.equals()` method instead of the `==` operator.

The `==` operator works only for primitive data types, such as `int`, `double`, and `char`, where it compares the values directly. However, for objects, including strings, it compares the references, which may not always be what you want.

For example, consider the following code:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";

if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("Strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("Strings are not equal");
}
```

In this case, since both `str1` and `str2` refer to the same string object, the `==` operator will return `true`. However, if you create two different string objects with the same value, like this:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = new String("hello");

if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("Strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("Strings are not equal");
}
```

Now, the `==` operator will return `false`, even though the two strings have the same value.

On the other hand, the `.equals()` method will correctly compare the values of the two strings, regardless of whether they refer to the same object or not:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = new String("hello");

if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("Strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("Strings are not equal");
}
```

In this case, the output will be "Strings are equal".

Therefore, when comparing strings in Java, it is recommended to use the `.equals()` method, as it provides a more reliable and consistent way of comparing string values.

